      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784119777843,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Notes\n\nMust know about Video Streaming\n\n1. Video Codec\n- A video codec compresses and decompresses digital video, making it more efficient for storage and transmission.\n- abbreviation for \"encoder/decoder\", attempts to reduce size of a video while preserving quality\n- Codec tradeoffs on following\n    - Time required to compress\n    - support on different platforms\n    - compression efficiency (how much original file is reduced in size)\n    - compression quality (lossy or not)\n- eg. H.264, H.265 (HEVC), VP9, AV1, MPEG-2, and MPEG-4.\n\n\n2. Video Container\n-  A file format that stores video data(frames, audio) and metadata. Stores video transcripts as well.\n- A codec determines how a video is compressed/decompressed, whereas container dictates file format for how the video is stored.\n- it varies by device/OS\n\n\n3. Bitrate\n- The number of bits transmitted over a period of time, measured in kbps or Mbps\n- size and quality of video affect the bitrate\n- High resolution video with higher framerates have higher bitrates, because there's literally more data to be transferred to play the video\n\n\n4. Manifest Files\n- Text-based documents giving details about video streams.\n- two types of manifest\n    - Primary : list all the available versions of a video(the different formats), primary is the \"root\" file pointing to media manifest files, each representing a different version of the video\n        A video is split into smaller segments, each a few seconds long\n    - Media : This manifest list out the links of these clip files and are used by video player to stream video by serving as an \"index\" to these segments.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Notes\n\nMust know about Video Streaming\n\n1. Video Codec\n- A video codec compresses and decompresses digital video, making it more efficient for storage and transmission.\n- abbreviation for \"encoder/decoder\", attempts to reduce size of a video while preserving quality\n- Codec tradeoffs on following\n    - Time required to compress\n    - support on different platforms\n    - compression efficiency (how much original file is reduced in size)\n    - compression quality (lossy or not)\n- eg. H.264, H.265 (HEVC), VP9, AV1, MPEG-2, and MPEG-4.\n\n\n2. Video Container\n-  A file format that stores video data(frames, audio) and metadata. Stores video transcripts as well.\n- A codec determines how a video is compressed/decompressed, whereas container dictates file format for how the video is stored.\n- it varies by device/OS\n\n\n3. Bitrate\n- The number of bits transmitted over a period of time, measured in kbps or Mbps\n- size and quality of video affect the bitrate\n- High resolution video with higher framerates have higher bitrates, because there's literally more data to be transferred to play the video\n\n\n4. Manifest Files\n- Text-based documents giving details about video streams.\n- two types of manifest\n    - Primary : list all the available versions of a video(the different formats), primary is the \"root\" file pointing to media manifest files, each representing a different version of the video\n        A video is split into smaller segments, each a few seconds long\n    - Media : This manifest list out the links of these clip files and are used by video player to stream video by serving as an \"index\" to these segments.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "A4GRtgwKPBvPCxIkEScem",
