      "locked": false,
      "text": "- ~1M videos/day = 365M records in DB to store metadata consider a horizontally partitioned DB say DynamoDB or Cassandra, partition on videoId\n- For actual video use AWS S3(Simple Storage Service) via presigned URL with multi-part upload",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- ~1M videos/day = 365M records in DB to store metadata consider a horizontally partitioned DB say DynamoDB or Cassandra, partition on videoId\n- For actual video use AWS S3(Simple Storage Service) via presigned URL with multi-part upload",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "eIceYOUIVVoaN_W3qMx8X",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -33759.443655879004,
      "y": -4169.355594167502,
      "width": 1824.9385986328125,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1396900748,
      "version": 1023,
      "versionNonce": 1763198132,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784120455320,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to store video in S3?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Store the raw video\n- store the video as is in S3 without any post-processing\nProblem : Different videos require different video formats(240p...4K), this approach misses that\n\n2. Good Solution : Store different Video formats\n- after uploading the video to S3 do a post processing to convert to different supported formats\n- once uploaded to S3, fire an event notification to video processing service, and once done processing it stores each format as a file in S3\n- Also updates video metadata record with the file URLs that represents different formats.\nProblem : Need to store videos in small segments so seamless in playing\n\n3. Great Solution : Store different Video formats as segments\n- This approach post-processes videos by splitting them into small segments (each a playable unit that's a few seconds in length) and then converts each segment into different formats.\n- This enabled more efficient video streaming ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to store video in S3?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Store the raw video\n- store the video as is in S3 without any post-processing\nProblem : Different videos require different video formats(240p...4K), this approach misses that\n\n2. Good Solution : Store different Video formats\n- after uploading the video to S3 do a post processing to convert to different supported formats\n- once uploaded to S3, fire an event notification to video processing service, and once done processing it stores each format as a file in S3\n- Also updates video metadata record with the file URLs that represents different formats.\nProblem : Need to store videos in small segments so seamless in playing\n\n3. Great Solution : Store different Video formats as segments\n- This approach post-processes videos by splitting them into small segments (each a playable unit that's a few seconds in length) and then converts each segment into different formats.\n- This enabled more efficient video streaming ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "LE79-KP4VnNw131S3w9zV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -33753.018995911116,
      "y": -3693.23678851738,
      "width": 2103.19873046875,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 147672076,
      "version": 1088,
      "versionNonce": 1429645068,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784121134490,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Users watching the videos?\n- Client fetches the VideoMetadata from DynamoDB\n\n1. Bad Solution : Download the entire video file\n- client downloads the whole video using S3 download link then plays it, so it isn't streaming rather is download and play\nProblems : Huge latency in playing, if download fails re-downloads it to play\n\n2. Good Solution : Download segments incrementally\n- incrementally download video segments(few seconds clip) that we created in S3 based on client's chosen video format\n- Load the first segment quickly and while it plays download the next segments in background\nProblem : Streaming disrupted if poor network connectivity and leads to lots of buffering\n\n3. Great Solution : Adaptive bitrate streaming\n- Relies on stored segments of video in different formats, manifest file being created during video upload time referencing all video segments for all available formats.\n- The client will fetch the VideoMetadata, which will have a URL pointing to the manifest file in S3.\n- The client will download the manifest file.\n- The client will choose a format based on network conditions / user settings. The client retrieves the URL for this segment in its chosen format from the manifest file. The client will download the first segment.\n- The client will play that segment and begin downloading more segments.\n- If the client detects that network conditions are slowing down (or improving), it will vary the format of the video segments it is downloading. If network conditions get worse (e.g. the bitrate is lower), the client \n  will attempt to download more compressed, lower resolution segments to avoid any interruption in streaming.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Users watching the videos?\n- Client fetches the VideoMetadata from DynamoDB\n\n1. Bad Solution : Download the entire video file\n- client downloads the whole video using S3 download link then plays it, so it isn't streaming rather is download and play\nProblems : Huge latency in playing, if download fails re-downloads it to play\n\n2. Good Solution : Download segments incrementally\n- incrementally download video segments(few seconds clip) that we created in S3 based on client's chosen video format\n- Load the first segment quickly and while it plays download the next segments in background\nProblem : Streaming disrupted if poor network connectivity and leads to lots of buffering\n\n3. Great Solution : Adaptive bitrate streaming\n- Relies on stored segments of video in different formats, manifest file being created during video upload time referencing all video segments for all available formats.\n- The client will fetch the VideoMetadata, which will have a URL pointing to the manifest file in S3.\n- The client will download the manifest file.\n- The client will choose a format based on network conditions / user settings. The client retrieves the URL for this segment in its chosen format from the manifest file. The client will download the first segment.\n- The client will play that segment and begin downloading more segments.\n- If the client detects that network conditions are slowing down (or improving), it will vary the format of the video segments it is downloading. If network conditions get worse (e.g. the bitrate is lower), the client \n  will attempt to download more compressed, lower resolution segments to avoid any interruption in streaming.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "OQ1HO4lJyz0O8AFk6Ke3j",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -31659.619902193786,
      "y": -5823.838998827177,
      "width": 206.72721259086873,
      "height": 47.676976413593366,
