    {
      "id": "Qd9uhtMe1isDp5xc8gysl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -31655.199606035414,
      "y": -5643.7126721247305,
      "width": 2033.57861328125,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1233625908,
      "version": 1120,
      "versionNonce": 1693969164,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784121696784,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle video processing to support adaptive bitrate streaming?\n- Auto resolution in YT is this where client based on network condition downloads the higher/lower resolution segments from S3\n- Once video uploaded to S3 by client its sent to a post-processing pipeline, which outputs\n    - Video segments files in different formats (codec and container combinations) stored in S3\n    - Manifest files (primary + media) stored in S3\n- To generate segments and manifest files:\n    - Split up the original file into segments (using a tool like ffmpeg or similar). These segments will be transcoded (converted from one encoding to another) and used to generated different video containers.\n    - Transcode (convert from one encoding to another) each segment and process other aspects of the segments (audio, transcript generation).\n    - Create manifest files referencing the different segments in different video formats.\n    - Mark upload as \"complete\"\n\n- Since the original video is uploaded in chunks, Most production system starts processing on the chunks uploaded while the client is still uploading rest of the chunks.\n- Segment processing to be done in parallel on different worker nodes as there's no dependency b/w segments.\n- Most time taking and costly operation is video segment transcoding, all this work can be imagined as DAG, and an orchestrator like Temporal can be used.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle video processing to support adaptive bitrate streaming?\n- Auto resolution in YT is this where client based on network condition downloads the higher/lower resolution segments from S3\n- Once video uploaded to S3 by client its sent to a post-processing pipeline, which outputs\n    - Video segments files in different formats (codec and container combinations) stored in S3\n    - Manifest files (primary + media) stored in S3\n- To generate segments and manifest files:\n    - Split up the original file into segments (using a tool like ffmpeg or similar). These segments will be transcoded (converted from one encoding to another) and used to generated different video containers.\n    - Transcode (convert from one encoding to another) each segment and process other aspects of the segments (audio, transcript generation).\n    - Create manifest files referencing the different segments in different video formats.\n    - Mark upload as \"complete\"\n\n- Since the original video is uploaded in chunks, Most production system starts processing on the chunks uploaded while the client is still uploading rest of the chunks.\n- Segment processing to be done in parallel on different worker nodes as there's no dependency b/w segments.\n- Most time taking and costly operation is video segment transcoding, all this work can be imagined as DAG, and an orchestrator like Temporal can be used.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Pgcl6JDwl-SU3WVZaOij8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -31648.475542518576,
      "y": -5217.330949605519,
      "width": 341.09979248046875,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
